Subject: register VM argument staging uses an incremental index

What this does:
  `regvm_arg_retarget` decided whether a call argument's producer may write
  the call window directly by scanning the whole IR for the value's definition
  and for any non-destination read of it. Every call argument did that scan, so
  lowering was quadratic in the IR size and dominated encode time on large
  functions (pdf.js).

  The pass now keeps `aw_def` (last destination write per value), `aw_reads`
  (non-destination `r` operand read count) and `aw_last_win` (last instruction
  naming the call window) in `JSRegIR`, folded lazily by `regvm_arg_index_sync`
  as instructions are appended. The per-argument test is O(1). The six call
  lowerings that retarget a producer into the window go through
  `regvm_arg_retarget_insn`, which keeps the index exact. A value outside the
  index (allocation failed, or beyond it) falls back to the original full scan
  `regvm_arg_retarget_scan`, and `QJS_REGVM_ARGCHECK` (debug) cross-checks the
  fast path against that scan and aborts on a mismatch.

Why we need it:
  Encode time on pdf.js and other large inputs was dominated by this quadratic
  scan; the register VM has to lower big functions in reasonable time.

Needs a bytecode version bump:
  No. Lowering only; the emitted bytecode is unchanged.

